# No Nested Types

Do not declare classes, interfaces, records, or enums inside another class or interface. Every type gets its own top-level file in the same package.

This includes:

- Non-static inner classes
- Static nested classes
- Local classes (declared inside a method)
- Nested enums

```java
// ❌ BAD — nested class and enum in Town.java
public class Town {
    public enum TownStatus { ACTIVE, RUINED }

    private static class TownCache {
        // ...
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD — split into separate files
// Town.java
public class Town { /* ... */ }

// TownStatus.java
public enum TownStatus { ACTIVE, RUINED }

// TownCache.java
class TownCache { /* package-private or public as appropriate */ }
```

```java
// ❌ BAD — local class inside a method
public void process() {
    class Helper { void run() { } }
    new Helper().run();
}

// ✅ GOOD — top-level Helper.java (or a named utility class)
public void process() {
    new Helper().run();
}
```

## Naming and Visibility

- Use the same package as the parent type unless there is a strong reason to hide it elsewhere.
- Prefer package-private for types only used within the package; use `public` when other packages need access.
- Name files to match the type (`TownStatus.java` for `enum TownStatus`).

## When Editing Existing Code

If you touch code that contains a nested type, extract it to a new file in the same change rather than adding more nesting.

## Anonymous Classes

Avoid new anonymous classes when a named top-level type or a lambda would be clearer. Do not grow anonymous classes into multi-method mini-types—extract them instead.
